{
  "requirement": "Habeas's bundled agent instructions MUST direct agents in Habeas-enabled Cargo\nprojects to use the coverage workflow of\n{cite}`requirements.test-coverage.run` by default for Rust unit and integration\ntest runs, including focused or filtered runs. That workflow MUST execute tests\nthrough `cargo nextest`.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "structural: `requirements.test-coverage.guidance` has no structural obligation beyond the content of the bundled instructions. The text names no library, signature, type, field, algorithm or named interface to build. Its clauses are:\n\n- \"bundled agent instructions MUST direct agents … to use the coverage workflow of `requirements.test-coverage.run` by default for Rust unit and integration test runs, including focused or filtered runs\". This is about what the instructions say. Any wording or file layout that tells agents this satisfies it, so those choices are discretionary.\n- \"That workflow MUST execute tests through `cargo nextest`\". This describes how `habeas coverage` behaves, which `requirements.test-coverage.run` covers. It is not a structural obligation on the instructions.\n\nThe four supplied roots are already linked as implementing the requirement, so evidence is present. I read the following passages:\n\n- `assets/skills/habeas/SKILL.md`, section `Testing and coverage artifacts`: \"Use `habeas coverage` by default for every Rust unit and integration test run, including focused runs (`habeas coverage -- -E 'test(name)'`). The workflow uses `cargo nextest`…\".\n- `assets/skills/habeas-evidence/SKILL.md` and `assets/skills/habeas-bootstrap-requirements/SKILL.md`: both direct agents to \"default to `habeas coverage` for unit, integration and focused runs through `cargo nextest`\".\n- `agent-sops/approved-implementation.sop.md`: \"Use `cargo habeas coverage -- <nextest arguments>` for every Rust unit and integration test run, including focused or filtered runs. It executes parallel processes through `cargo nextest`…\".\n\nNo further implementation roots are needed.\n\nbehavioral: The subject has two behavioral obligations about the text of the bundled agent instructions: (1) they direct agents to use `habeas coverage` by default for Rust unit and integration test runs, including focused or filtered runs, and (2) that workflow executes tests through `cargo nextest`.\n\n`tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[bundled_skills_default_to_process_coverage_and_keep_artifacts_disposable]` is marked `{test}` for the subject. It covers both obligations.\n\n- **Skills covered.** For each skill that mentions running tests (`habeas`, `habeas-evidence`, `habeas-bootstrap-requirements`), it collapses whitespace and asserts the text contains `habeas coverage`, `cargo nextest`, `focused runs`, and \"by default\" or \"default to\".\n- **Home skill.** For `habeas`, it asserts the exact directive: \"Use `habeas coverage` by default for every Rust unit and integration test run, including focused runs (`habeas coverage -- -E 'test(name)'`)\". It also asserts \"The workflow uses `cargo nextest` for parallel process runs…\". Together these cover the by-default and focused/filtered obligation and the nextest obligation.\n- **Other two skills.** It asserts both say \"default to `habeas coverage` for unit, integration and focused runs through `cargo nextest`\".\n\nI searched `assets/skills` and found no instruction in `habeas-refactor` or `habeas-requirements` about running tests. The skills the test covers are therefore the ones that carry the obligation. The test also asserts the `agent-sops/approved-implementation.sop.md` text. Whether the SOP counts as a bundled instruction is unclear, but it is extra coverage either way.\n\nThe requirement is about instruction text, so text assertions are the appropriate way to test it. I found no uncovered obligation or boundary.",
